---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { SITE } from '../lib/site'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — The Reel Vault"
  description="The Reel Vault is a curated picture house for classic public-domain film, drawing on the Internet Archive and Wikipedia."
>
  <div class="section-head">
    <div><span class="kicker">Who we are</span><h2>About The Reel Vault</h2></div>
  </div>

  <article class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p>
      <strong>The Reel Vault</strong> is a curated picture house for the films time forgot.
      The twentieth century left behind an extraordinary quantity of motion pictures that
      have since entered the public domain — feature films whose copyrights lapsed,
      newsreels, cartoons, educational shorts and the everyday ephemera of a hundred years
      of life. Most of it sits, freely and legally available, in the great open library of
      the <a href="https://archive.org" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Internet Archive</a>.
      It is wonderful, and it is overwhelming.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our purpose is curation. Rather than dump a search box in front of you, we organise
      this material into genres and categories, write guided <a href="/collections">collections</a>
      that put the films in context, and give every title its own page with the story behind
      it. We want the experience to feel less like trawling a database and more like an
      evening at a thoughtful repertory cinema.
    </p>

    <h3>Where the films and facts come from</h3>
    <p>
      Every film streams directly from the Internet Archive, which hosts and serves the
      video. We do not host the films ourselves. The background information on each title is
      drawn first from <a href="https://wikipedia.org" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Wikipedia</a>,
      and where no article exists we fall back to the description supplied by the Internet
      Archive. We always tell you which source you are reading, and link back to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The curation, the collections, the editorial writing and the design are our own.
      The films and the encyclopaedic facts belong to their creators and to the public, and
      we are grateful to the Internet Archive and to Wikipedia's contributors for making
      them available.
    </p>

    <h3>Your data stays with you</h3>
    <p>
      The Reel Vault has no accounts and no server-side database. Your favourites, your
      viewing history and your place in a half-watched film are stored only in your own
      browser. We never see them. You can read the details in our
      <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>A note on copyright</h3>
    <p>
      We aim to surface material that is in the public domain or otherwise free to view.
      Copyright status can be complicated, and we rely in part on the metadata provided by
      our sources. If you hold rights to a work and believe it is being made available in
      error, please see our <a href="/terms">Terms</a> and
      <a href="/contact">contact us</a> — we respond promptly to legitimate requests.
    </p>

    <p style="margin-top:28px">
      Questions, suggestions or a film we simply have to feature? We would love to hear from
      you at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
